### 🟦 LinkedIn Post (The "Pragmatic Senior" Angle)

**Strategy:** Start with a bold hook that challenges typical tutorials. Use a screenshot of code (code snippets perform exceptionally well on LinkedIn).

**The Post:** Most API design guides stop at "use nouns, not verbs."

That is fine for Day 1. But it doesn't prepare you for Day 100—when your DB is under load, clients are retrying aggressively, and a breaking change has to land without taking down your users.

An API is a promise. Every rough edge is a promise you’ll have to keep forever.

I just wrote a breakdown of the 5 backend API design tips I wish someone had drilled into me earlier. These are the things that separate "tutorial APIs" from "production-ready APIs":

1️⃣ **Killing the N+1 Problem:** If you are looping and calling the database inside the loop, you have a problem. Bulk fetch instead. 2️⃣ **Cursor vs. Offset Pagination:** Why offset pagination breaks under concurrent writes, and when to use cursors. 3️⃣ **Idempotency Keys:** How to stop a network timeout from double-charging a user. 4️⃣ **RFC 9457 for Errors:** Designing errors as first-class citizens so clients can actually handle them. 5️⃣ **Day-1 Versioning:** Version before you have a breaking change, not after.

Link to the full deep-dive is in the comments! 👇

What is the one API design mistake that haunts your production logs?

